          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How is COPD different from asthma?",
          "answer": "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) and asthma are both chronic respiratory conditions, but they have distinct characteristics and management approaches.\n\n**COPD** is characterized by persistent airflow obstruction that is not fully reversible. This condition typically results from long-term exposure to harmful substances, most commonly tobacco smoke. Symptoms include chronic cough, sputum production, and shortness of breath that usually worsen over time. COPD is diagnosed using lung function tests, such as spirometry ([source](https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/data/icd/ICD10CM_Coding_Reference_2021.pdf)).\n\n**Asthma**, on the other hand, is marked by airway inflammation and increased responsiveness to various triggers. This condition leads to variable airflow obstruction that can be reversible, showcasing symptoms like wheezing, chest tightness, and cough that may vary in intensity and can worsen at night or with specific triggers. Although both conditions can coexist, the key difference is that asthma symptoms are often more variable and can improve with asthma medication ([source](https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/data/icd/ICD10CM_Coding_Reference_2021.pdf)).\n\nIn summary, COPD is defined by progressive and irreversible airflow limitation, primarily caused by long-term exposure to irritants, while asthma is characterized by reversible airflow obstruction with variability in symptoms.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
